🚽 Test case: round-13 review finding #3 -- buf[0] (NODE_ARRAY_ACCESS on a
🚽 char array) is an ordinary char lvalue in C, the same as any other char
🚽 expression; `takes_char(buf[0])` must type-check successfully as a char
🚽 argument, not be rejected. C only promotes a char argument to int for an
🚽 unprototyped call or the variadic tail of a variadic one (default
🚽 argument promotions) -- never merely because the argument expression
🚽 happens to be an array subscript passed to a normally-prototyped
🚽 parameter, which is every Brainrot native call. stdrot_char_narrows_
🚽 to_int() (stdrot.h) previously narrowed NODE_ARRAY_ACCESS unconditionally
🚽 to int -- both the static checker and the runtime marshaller agreed with
🚽 each other, but both were wrong, and a prior round's tests enshrined
🚽 that agreement as "correct" behavior. Fixed on both sides; this fixture
🚽 replaces the old semantic_error_native_char_array_access.brainrot, which
🚽 asserted the rejection this fix removes.
skibidi main {
    yap buf[32];
    slorp(buf);
    rizz r = takes_char(buf[0]);
    yapping("%d", r);
    bussin 0;
}
